I've ascended a gnomish healer. Not pacifist the whole time, only 
semi-pacifist for the Minetown Protection Run (MPR)

>How to you get Elbereth to work reliably while only being able to write
>in the dust and not really engrave?  It only seems to work half the
>time.

You don't Elbereth isn't your primary protection. Your pet is. You spend
time growing it to XL9 with 72 HP. At that point it's a fast tough killing
machine. Only downside is that it stops to eat its kills. 

You have a stethoscope. So you can always monitor its health. You have the
ability to cast healing spells on yourself and your pet.

>Which actions will not rub out dust writing? (I had thought any action
>rubbed it out, but I opened a door while standing on Elbereth and it
>didn't rub it out.)

No matter. Elbereth isn't the primary tool. Though it's good to be able to
use once you get a hard gem or a durable wand (fire, lightning, or to some
extent digging).

>It this tactic meant to be passafist all game or just until you get
>protection from Minetown?  (It seems if I've only assenced once before,
>I shouldn't be taking extra challenges yet).

I use it for the MPR. The extra AC weighs heavily in the character's
favor as they start to level up. So does the buff pet as a partner. It
gets even better if you can poly the pet into something really big mean
and nasty. In my last game I had a Balrog. But it was too slow to keep.

>I keep accedently bumping newts or grid bugs and killing them. 

Part of that being careful. I usually kill newts on purpose and eat them
to see if I can get an extra power point or two.

> So I
>started weilding nothing to do less damage when I accedently hit, but
>I've still killed a newt that way.  Passafist in nethack is only not
>killing monsters, right?  (I mean, can you stay passifist if you hit but
>don't kill?)

Right. But for the MPR you really just trying to slow down the rate at
which you acquire experience. And as you've found out, you'll bump up
your XPs simply by running into monsters. Gnome zombies are the worst.
8 points of XPs and usually be killed in a single blow.

>I was thinking disarming traps gave exp but I did it and no exp, so what
>gives exp besides killing monsters? (I know I've seen something telling
>about this before, but while I'm asking passafist questions I might as
>well ask this).

Of course potions of gain level are the primary method of gaining experience.
The next one is talking to the Oracle. Also you can manage gains in both power
points and hit points by a variety of methods.

But I always run it as a temp measure for the MPR.

